<!--
    GH-831: RISC-V fmv.x.d of a call result prints as dat_900.

    fmv.x.d read its source through *[register] at the address of the frs1D
    operand, but frs1D exports a temporary copy, so the read was register
    offset 0x900 (no RISC-V register) instead of the FP register.  fmv.x.w
    had the same read, and it and fcvt.w.d / fcvt.wu.d / fcvt.w.s / fcvt.wu.s
    wrote their result through rdW, which also exports a temporary: the
    integer destination was never written, so these instructions had no
    effect at all.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 p-code lift (RISC-V SLEIGH spec fix, no option).
    The RISC-V .sla files must be rebuilt from the edited .sinc files.

    Source (clang -O2 -mno-relax -fno-optimize-sibling-calls
    -target riscv64-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes verbatim below):

        static double gd(int k) { return k; }            (noinline)
        static float gf(int k) { return k; }             (noinline)
        static long ui(long a, long b) { return a ^ b; } (noinline)
        long e13(long k) { double t = gd(k); long b;
                           memcpy(&b, &t, 8); return ui(k, b); }
        long dbits(double d) { long l; memcpy(&l, &d, 8); return l; }
        int fwplus(int k) { float t = gf(k); int b;
                            memcpy(&b, &t, 4); return b + 1; }
        int cvtw(double d) { return (int)d; }
        unsigned cvtwu(double d) { return (unsigned)d; }
        int cvtws(float f) { return (int)f; }
        unsigned cvtwus(float f) { return (unsigned)f; }

    #1-#3: the fmv.x.d / fmv.x.w results are the FP register's bits (the
    call result in e13 and fwplus).  #4-#5: the fcvt.w* results reach a0
    (the unsigned forms convert through a 64-bit integer, so values in
    [2^31, 2^32) keep their bits).  #6-#7: neither the 0x900 read nor a
    void conversion function remains.
-->
